% === provenance: Marton2015 ===
% CLAIM: Contrast must be experienced simultaneously (instances
%   juxtaposed), not reconstructed from memory, for the varying aspect to
%   be discerned; and a task that points out the critical aspects leaves
%   the students nothing to discern (the teacher's paradox).
% FOUND-VIA: inherited from
%   modules/computational-thinking/slides/ltnotes.bib, where it backs the
%   same variation-pattern notes.
% FOUND-VIA (here): backed in Algoritmiskt tänkande, bilaga A;
%   provenance block copied verbatim from modules/packages/slides/
%   ltnotes.bib, which copied it from that deck.
% PICKED: the primary book-length statement of variation theory's
%   necessary conditions, used course-wide in ltnotes.
% QUOTE (pp. 45--47): "if you cannot see the greenness of any of three
%   green, though otherwise different, things, you cannot see what they
%   have in common, either" (p. 46); of two teachers juxtaposing objects,
%   "[o]ne is betting on induction, the other on contrast. The latter
%   wins" (p. 47); discernment comes from "the simultaneous experience of
%   things that differ in color but are the same otherwise" (p. 46).
% QUOTE (p. 13): "the more clearly the teacher tells the students what is
%   to be done, the less chance the students get to make the necessary
%   distinctions (for instance, between what is critical and what is
%   not)."
% QUOTE (p. 91): "[T]he questions should not point out the relevant
%   aspects of the problem to be solved, as this is exactly what the
%   students are supposed to find out (discern)."
% VERIFIED: pp. 45--47 and p. 13 quotes as recorded in the
%   variation-theory and try-first-tell-later skills (verified there
%   against the book); Routledge lists the print year as 2014, the 2015
%   year is kept for key-consistency with the other decks' ltnotes.bib.
% COUNTER: not re-run here; the claim is a course-wide methodological
%   premise already carried by the earlier decks.
% VERIFIED (reMarkable, 2026-09-04): re-read in the user's own copy of the
%   book on the reMarkable (/VT/NCoL/Necessary Conditions of Learning);
%   the three pp. 45--47 quotes stand on printed pp. 46 ("if you cannot
%   see the greenness ..."; "the simultaneous experience of things that
%   differ in color but are the same otherwise") and 47 ("[o]ne is betting
%   on induction, the other on contrast. The latter wins"), read off the
%   running heads.  The same page (46) adds that which arrangement counts
%   as contrast depends on the object of learning -- "we have to see this
%   question from the point of view of what is to be learned".  Where a
%   deck cites p. 13, that sentence is confirmed too ("the more clearly
%   the teacher tells the students what is to be done, the less chance
%   the students get to make the necessary distinctions"), framed by
%   Brousseau's didactic contract and stated for teacher and students
%   alike.
% VERIFIED (here): pp. 13 and 91 are cited here for the rule that a
%   question must not point out the aspect the student is to discern; the
%   p. 91 wording is the one recorded in the try-first-tell-later skill,
%   which states that pp. 13, 89 and 91 were verified against the printed
%   book on 2026-09-06.  NOT re-verified from this environment.
% DATE (here): 2026-09-17
@book{Marton2015,
  author    = {Marton, Ference},
  title     = {Necessary Conditions of Learning},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  address   = {London},
  year      = {2015},
  isbn      = {978-0-415-739139}
}


