% === provenance: PythonDataStructures ===
% CLAIM: The container behaviour the deck presents is Python's documented
%   behaviour: the list methods (append, extend, insert, remove, pop,
%   sort, copy, count, index), list comprehensions, the advice not to
%   change a list while looping over it, that a set has no duplicates,
%   that tuples are immutable, and that dictionaries are indexed by keys.
% FOUND-VIA (here): known-item, primary source.  The claim is about what
%   Python's containers do, so the language's own manual is the primary
%   source; a literature search would be the wrong instrument.  The
%   provenance block is copied from
%   modules/containers/slides-dicts/ltnotes.bib, where the same entry
%   backs the same behaviour; this deck cites it for the set's lack of
%   duplicates (\cref{sec:epost}) and for a key having to be immutable
%   (\cref{sec:brak}).
% PICKED: docs.python.org is the reference implementation's own tutorial,
%   chosen over third-party tutorials that paraphrase it.
% QUOTE (5.1 More on Lists): "Remove the item at the given position in the
%   list, and return it. If no index is specified, a.pop() removes and
%   returns the last item in the list. It raises an IndexError if the list
%   is empty or the index is outside the list range."  And, on sorting in
%   place: "Sort the items of the list in place (the arguments can be used
%   for sort customization, see sorted() for their explanation)."
%   (5.6 Looping Techniques): "It is sometimes tempting to change a list
%   while you are looping over it; however, it is often simpler and safer
%   to create a new list instead."
%   (5.1.3 List Comprehensions): "List comprehensions provide a concise
%   way to create lists."
%   (5.4 Sets): "A set is an unordered collection with no duplicate
%   elements."
%   (5.3 Tuples and Sequences): "Tuples are immutable, and usually contain
%   a heterogeneous sequence of elements that are accessed via unpacking
%   [...] or indexing".
%   (5.5 Dictionaries): "Unlike sequences, which are indexed by a range of
%   numbers, dictionaries are indexed by keys, which can be any immutable
%   type; strings and numbers can always be keys."
% VERIFIED (here): full text of
%   https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html read 2026-09-03
%   (version 3.14.7); every quoted sentence appears there verbatim under
%   the section given.  NOT re-fetched for this deck.
% COUNTER: not applicable -- the source defines the behaviour it is cited
